WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.667
Failure analysis: read the disagreements.

2
00:00:02.947 --> 00:00:05.528
The numbers say how often each system is wrong.

3
00:00:05.808 --> 00:00:09.883
Only reading the errors says why, and the why decides what you do next.

4
00:00:10.163 --> 00:00:24.179
Pull every case where the systems disagree, plus every case where both are wrong, and label each error with a short category: missed item, invented item, wrong owner, date arithmetic, priority convention, schema failure, and so on.

5
00:00:24.459 --> 00:00:29.877
This is the error analysis loop from Studio 3, lesson 2, applied to a comparison.

6
00:00:30.157 --> 00:00:36.429
Patterns you are likely to see in a fine-tuning comparison: Convention errors favor the fine-tuned model.

7
00:00:36.709 --> 00:00:49.168
If priority follows a house rule (“anything a client is waiting on is high”), a model trained on hundreds of labeled examples learns the rule; a frontier model with a few-shot prompt has to infer it.

8
00:00:49.448 --> 00:00:53.715
This is the narrow, learnable kind of task where fine-tuning tends to win.

9
00:00:53.995 --> 00:00:57.429
Judgment and world-knowledge errors favor the frontier model.

10
00:00:57.709 --> 00:01:06.285
Ambiguous ownership, unusual phrasing, or a snippet unlike anything in training is where a larger, more general model holds up better.

11
00:01:06.565 --> 00:01:10.896
Schema failures cluster in small models unless decoding is constrained.

12
00:01:11.176 --> 00:01:18.024
If most of the fine-tuned model’s errors are parse failures, the fix is in serving (lesson 11), not training.

13
00:01:18.304 --> 00:01:20.821
Shared errors point at the labels or the task.

14
00:01:21.101 --> 00:01:25.453
If every system gets a case wrong in the same way, read the gold label again.

15
00:01:25.733 --> 00:01:36.400
Sometimes the label is wrong, and sometimes the snippet is genuinely ambiguous and should be marked as such in the next version of the test set (never silently edited in this one).

16
00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:39.837
Report a small table of error categories by system.

17
00:01:40.117 --> 00:01:49.120
It is the most persuasive part of the write-up for a reader who doesn’t trust aggregate scores, and it tells you whether a second round of training data would help.
